; host/launcher.bl — the pinned HOST MODULE for `Elixir.BeamLisp.Launcher`.
;
; This file is not a namespace. It is a `.bl` SOURCE that the bootstrap seed
; compiles with `BeamLisp.AOT/compile_host_file` into ONE exact-named beam
; (`Elixir.BeamLisp.Launcher.beam`) whose top-level `defn`s are plain exported
; functions. It is the `.bl` port of the Elixir boot program that `bin/bl`
; used to feed to `elixir -e` — the program that installed the pinned hosts,
; judged whether the boot tier in `build/` was this tree's generation,
; rebuilt it in a CHILD VM when it was not, re-exec'd `bin/bl` under
; `BL_BOOT_REEXECED`, started the application (or the language alone for
; `deps`), ensured the native crates, and finally dispatched `bl.cli`
; `run-argv`.
;
; The ports live per step: the host install/manifest/floor are
; `Elixir.BeamLisp.Bootstrap`, the toolchain key is
; `Elixir.BeamLisp.AOTCache`, the beam provenance is `Elixir.BeamLisp.AOT`,
; the boot-tier membership is `Elixir.BeamLisp.Tiers`, and the search path,
; var table and loader are `Elixir.BeamLisp.Env` / `Elixir.BeamLisp.Loader`.
; What was left in `bin/bl` was the SEQUENCE — and this module is it.
;
; ── WHY A PINNED MODULE AND NOT A NAMESPACE ─────────────────────────────────
;
; The launcher runs FIRST: before `install_hosts!/1`, before the floor is
; staged, before a single `BeamLisp.Ns.*` beam is loadable on a cold tree. It
; is invoked from a trampoline (`elixir -e 'BeamLisp.Launcher.main()' -- …`,
; or an Erlang/Elixir entry point that calls `main/0` or `main/1`), so it
; cannot be an AOT-rebuilt namespace: a namespace's own function bodies resolve
; their siblings through the var table the launcher has not filled yet. A
; pinned host beam is a fixed artifact named by the seed manifest, built by one
; generation and used by the next.
;
; ── THE PREBOOT CONSTRAINT: NO Rt, NO core ──────────────────────────────────
;
; Everything on the launcher's path runs with nothing on the code path but
; Elixir/OTP, the pinned host leaves, and this beam. `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt` is not
; loaded and `core`'s vars are not interned — they are interned BY the boot
; this module enables. So the file is written in the surface the BEAM itself
; provides, exactly as `priv/host/aot_cache.bl` documents it:
;
;   * `erlang/*` BIFs — `apply`, `element`, `tuple_size`, `is_*`, `==`, `hd`,
;     `++`, `list_to_binary`, `integer_to_binary`, `function_exported`, … —
;     are remote calls to `:erlang`, free of any runtime;
;   * `lst`/`tup`/`bcat` build a list, a tuple and a binary from BIFs alone,
;     which is why they exist instead of `(list …)`, `(tuple …)` and `str`
;     (each of those lowers to a `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` call — measured);
;   * `Enum`, `Map`, `Keyword`, `Path`, `File`, `System`, `String`, `IO`,
;     `Code`, `Module`, `Macro`, `List` are host modules: Elixir/OTP, on the
;     path in any `elixir` VM, and the same modules the Elixir twin called;
;   * `cond`/`if`/`when`/`and`/`or`/`do` are MACROS — they expand at compile
;     time into `if`/`let`, which emit nothing at runtime;
;   * `case`, `(list …)`, `(tuple …)`, `str`, `nil?`, `{…}` map literals,
;     vector literals, destructuring, `for`/`doseq` and CALLING A LOCALLY-BOUND
;     FN are all AVOIDED: each emits a `BeamLisp.Ns.Core`/`Ns.Rt`/`Env`
;     reference (measured per-construct against the emitted atom table). The
;     seam calls below are therefore `call0/1/2/3`, which reach a function
;     VALUE through `erlang/apply` alone.
;
; `test/bl/launcher_host_test.bl` proves this twice: it asserts the emitted
; beam carries no `Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns.Core` atom, and it runs the preboot
; decisions in a fresh `elixir` child whose ONLY added code path entry is that
; beam — asserting `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt`, `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` and `BeamLisp.Env` are
; never loaded by them.
;
; ── THE SEAMS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The flow is effectful at its edges — the filesystem, the environment, two
; child processes, `System.halt`, and the language it boots. `boot/2` takes an
; optional map that overrides those edges; `default_seam/1` supplies the real
; implementation for every key. `main/1` passes an empty map, so production
; behaviour is the default one. The seams exist so the DECISIONS — which verb
; may boot without the application, whether the tier is current, which child
; command runs, which exit code is taken — are provable without booting the
; language or terminating a VM.
;
; ── THE CHILD REBUILD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; A stale tier is rebuilt in a CHILD VM because this parent must not boot the
; language itself: `ensure_all_started` starts `BeamLisp.Env`, and a pre-booted
; Env makes the application fail to start (`{:already_started,
; #PID<0.99.0>}`). The child's program is the one-line trampoline
; `BeamLisp.Launcher.rebuild_tier()` — the rebuild itself is `rebuild_tier/0`
; below, passed the ebin as `argv[0]`. It cannot run in the parent's VM for the
; reason the Elixir twin recorded: the parent already loaded modules from the
; OLD floor while judging the tier, and starting the application on a
; partially-reloaded image assembles a VM from two generations.
;
; (This wave owns source and test only; the seed entry that points the real
; atom at this file, the `bin/bl` cutover to `main/0`, and the deletion of the
; heredoc are the integrating wave's. The port is judged by its focused test,
; which compiles it at a probe atom and drives the flow through its seams.)
;
; Run: bin/bl test test/bl/launcher_host_test.bl

(ns host.launcher)

; ── the preboot constructors (BIFs alone; see the header) ───────────────────

(defn- lst
  "A plain list of the arguments. A rest argument IS a list, so this compiles
   to argument collection and nothing else — no `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` reference,
   which is what a bare `(list …)` would emit."
  [& xs] xs)

(defn- tup
  "A tuple of the arguments, built the way Erlang builds one. Replaces
   `(tuple …)`, which lowers to a `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` call."
  [& xs] (erlang/list_to_tuple xs))

(defn- bcat
  "A binary from the arguments (an iolist). Stands in for `str`/`Kernel.<>`,
   both of which compile to a runtime call."
  [& xs] (erlang/list_to_binary xs))

; ── reaching a function VALUE without the runtime ───────────────────────────
;
; `(f x)` where `f` is a local binding lowers to `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt.invoke`, which
; is `undef` before the floor boots. `erlang/apply` is a BIF and takes a fun
; directly, so every seam call goes through these four.

(defn- call0 [f] (erlang/apply f (lst)))
(defn- call1 [f a] (erlang/apply f (lst a)))
(defn- call2 [f a b] (erlang/apply f (lst a b)))
(defn- call3 [f a b c] (erlang/apply f (lst a b c)))

; ── the decisions (pure; no host module, no language) ───────────────────────

(defn provisioning_argv?
  "Whether `argv` is the one verb allowed to boot WITHOUT the application.
   `deps` installs the libraries the application needs, so requiring them to
   be present would be circular (measured on an empty cache:
   `{:error, {:x509, {~c\"no such file or directory\", ~c\"x509.app\"}}}`). The
   verb is matched at FIRST position only — `bl version deps` is not it."
  [argv]
  (erlang/== (Enum/take argv 1) (lst "deps")))

(defn tier_module
  "The module atom a boot namespace's beam is emitted at: `BeamLisp.Ns.` joined
   with the CAMELIZED dot segments of `ns`. This is the Elixir twin's
   `Module.concat([BeamLisp.Ns | Enum.map(String.split(ns, \".\"),
   &Macro.camelize/1)])`, and it is spelled here rather than asked of
   `BeamLisp.Link/module_for` on purpose: this verdict runs before `rt`/`core`
   load, and a previous-generation `Link` may use runtime helpers unavailable
   until the floor boots. Kebab segments are preserved: the live tree's module
   IS `Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns.Reader-node`."
  [ns]
  (Module/concat
    (erlang/++ (lst :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Ns")
               (Enum/map (String/split ns ".") (fn [part] (Macro/camelize part))))))

(defn provenance_key
  "The compiler key a beam's provenance carries, or nil when it carries none.
   `{hash, key}` is what `AOT.beam_provenance/1` answers; the `%{key: key}`
   clause keeps the older map shape answerable. An unanswerable question is not
   a yes."
  [p]
  (cond
    (erlang/is_map p) (Map/get p :key)

    (and (erlang/is_tuple p)
         (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size p))
         (erlang/is_binary (erlang/element 2 p)))
      (erlang/element 2 p)

    :else nil))

(defn tier_current?
  "Whether EVERY `{ns, provenance}` pair in `pairs` was compiled by `key_now`.
   One generation is the whole question: a tier that is half this tree and half
   the previous one is the state that produced this tree's worst failure, so a
   single mismatch is `false`."
  [pairs key_now]
  (Enum/all? pairs (fn [pair] (erlang/== key_now (provenance_key (erlang/element 2 pair))))))

(defn tier_verdict
  "The tier verdict the launcher acts on. A tree with no committed seed has
   nothing to compare against and is served as-is; with a seed, an unanswerable
   provenance question (`can_judge?` false — e.g. an older `AOT` without
   `beam_provenance/1`) is NOT a yes, so the tier rebuilds. That is always the
   safe direction."
  [manifest can_judge? pairs key_now]
  (or (erlang/== nil manifest)
      (and can_judge? (tier_current? pairs key_now))))

; ── the seams ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn default_seam
  "The real implementation of seam `k` — the effectful edge `boot/2` calls by
   default. Every branch builds a fun with host modules and BIFs only."
  [k]
  (cond
    (erlang/== k :get_env) (fn [name] (System/get_env name))
    (erlang/== k :stderr) (fn [msg] (IO/puts :stderr msg))
    (erlang/== k :halt) (fn [code] (System/halt code))
    (erlang/== k :cmd) (fn [exe args opts] (System/cmd exe args opts))
    (erlang/== k :code_path) (fn [] (code/get_path))
    (erlang/== k :find_executable) (fn [name] (System/find_executable name))
    (erlang/== k :install_hosts) (fn [ebin] (BeamLisp.Bootstrap/install_hosts! ebin))
    (erlang/== k :install_floor) (fn [ebin] (BeamLisp.Bootstrap/install! ebin))
    (erlang/== k :can_judge)
      (fn [] (and (Code/ensure_loaded? :"Elixir.BeamLisp.AOT")
                  (erlang/function_exported :"Elixir.BeamLisp.AOT" :beam_provenance 1)))
    (erlang/== k :build_tier) (fn [ebin root] (rebuild_source_tier ebin root))
    (erlang/== k :manifest) (fn [] (BeamLisp.Bootstrap/manifest))
    (erlang/== k :key_now) (fn [] (BeamLisp.AOTCache/current_compiler_key))
    (erlang/== k :provenance) (fn [mod] (BeamLisp.AOT/beam_provenance mod))
    (erlang/== k :boot_namespaces) (fn [] (BeamLisp.Tiers/boot_namespaces))
    (erlang/== k :start_app) (fn [] (Application/ensure_all_started :beam_lisp))
    (erlang/== k :load_app) (fn [] (Application/load :beam_lisp))
    (erlang/== k :aot_boot) (fn [] (BeamLisp.AOT/boot))
    (erlang/== k :ensure_loaded) (fn [ns] (BeamLisp.Loader/ensure_loaded ns))
    (erlang/== k :fetch) (fn [ns name] (BeamLisp.Env/fetch! ns name))
    (erlang/== k :invoke) (fn [f args] (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/invoke f args))

    :else
      (fn [& _args]
        (erlang/error
          (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception "unknown launcher seam")))))

(defn- seam [seams k] (Map/get seams k (default_seam k)))

; ── the preboot steps ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn boot_ebin
  "Where the beams are: `BL_EBIN` when the launcher was given one (the image
   directory `bin/bl` builds), else `build/` under the working directory."
  [seams]
  (or (call1 (seam seams :get_env) "BL_EBIN") (Path/expand "build")))

(defn boot_root
  "The TREE root the boot-tier rebuild reads its sources from. It is not the
   ebin's parent under a release layout, so `BL_ROOT` is consulted first — the
   variable `bin/bl` exports for exactly this question."
  [seams ebin]
  (or (call1 (seam seams :get_env) "BL_ROOT") (Path/dirname ebin)))

(defn path_string
  "A code-path entry as a string. `:code.get_path/0` answers charlists, and a
   seam may hand over binaries: accept both rather than pin the seam to one."
  [p]
  (if (erlang/is_binary p) p (List/to_string p)))

(defn child_pa
  "This VM's whole code path as `-pa` args for the child VM. Every path is
   named because the child stages the floor and then builds the tier from the
   tree: the same code path the parent booted on is the one the rebuild needs."
  [paths]
  ; `-pa` prepends. Reverse the parent's priority-ordered code path so the
  ; child sees the SAME winning beam when a pinned override precedes build/.
  (Enum/flat_map (Enum/reverse paths) (fn [p] (lst "-pa" (path_string p)))))

(defn build_program
  "The child VM's program, as Elixir source: the trampoline to the rebuild
   below. Passing a NAME, never a program body, is what keeps a quote in the
   rebuild from truncating a shell-quoted boot program — the trap the `bin/bl`
   heredoc records."
  []
  "BeamLisp.Launcher.rebuild_tier()")

(defn build_sources
  "The boot-tier sources, in the order the Elixir twin listed them: the flat
   `priv/boot/*.bl` run, the std multi-file prelude it requires, and the
   literate `.bl.md` / `.bl.org` sources."
  [root]
  (erlang/++
    (Path/wildcard (Path/join root "priv/boot/*.bl"))
    (erlang/++
      (Path/wildcard (Path/join root "priv/std/multi.bl"))
      (erlang/++
        (Path/wildcard (Path/join root "priv/boot/*.bl.md"))
        (Path/wildcard (Path/join root "priv/boot/*.bl.org"))))))

(defn build_args
  "The argument map `build/run` takes, key for key as the Elixir twin built it.
   `:log` streams the child's progress lines to stderr.

   `:scope :scoped` because this child owns a SUBSET: `build_sources` is
   `priv/boot/` plus the std prelude it requires, compiled into the SAME ebin
   the whole tree builds into. An authoritative full run handed a boot-only
   list would read every recorded source it was not handed as deleted, and
   drop the library's records and beams — the boot self-heal would demolish
   the build it is healing. Scoped says exactly what this child means: prune
   nothing it was not handed."
  [ebin root]
  (Map/put
    (Map/put
      (Map/put
        (Map/put
          (Map/put
            (Map/put
              (Map/put (Map/new) :sources (build_sources root))
              :out ebin)
            :manifest (Path/join ebin ".beam_lisp/compile.beam_lisp"))
          :force? false)
        :jobs nil)
      :log (fn [msg] (IO/puts :stderr msg)))
    :scope :scoped))

(defn rebuild_source_tier
  "Compile the boot tier as a unit after the child has installed its floor."
  [ebin root]
  (BeamLisp.AOT/boot)
  (BeamLisp.Env/add_search_path (Path/join root "priv/boot"))
  (BeamLisp.Env/add_search_path (Path/join root "priv/std"))
  (BeamLisp.Loader/ensure_loaded "build")
  (let [run (BeamLisp.Env/fetch! "build" "run")
        result (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/invoke run (lst (build_args ebin root)))
        errors (Map/get result :errors)]
    (if (erlang/== 0 (Enum/count errors))
      result
      (do
        (Enum/each errors (fn [message] (IO/puts :stderr message)))
        (System/halt 70)))))

(defn installed_tier_current?
  "A child may skip compilation only with affirmative installed-floor evidence.
   The parent's seedless-tree policy is deliberately not an affirmative child
   verdict. Bootstrap.install! has already verified the seed files; reread the
   live compiler key and every boot member after it replaces stale artifacts."
  [seams]
  (let [manifest (call0 (seam seams :manifest))
        key_now (call0 (seam seams :key_now))
        namespaces (call0 (seam seams :boot_namespaces))
        pairs (Enum/map namespaces
                (fn [ns] (tup ns (call1 (seam seams :provenance) (tier_module ns)))))
        can_judge? (call0 (seam seams :can_judge))]
    (and (erlang/is_map manifest)
         (erlang/is_binary key_now)
         (erlang/== key_now (Map/get manifest "compiler_key"))
         (erlang/not (erlang/== (lst) namespaces))
         can_judge?
         (tier_verdict manifest can_judge? pairs key_now))))

(defn rebuild_tier_at
  "Child-only sequence, with effect seams for tests. A compatible committed
   seed can repair stale build/ without recompiling the same boot sources.
   Source changes, mixed provenance and unavailable judgment still compile.
   Installation failures propagate before either a skip or a source build."
  [ebin root seams]
  (call1 (seam seams :install_floor) ebin)
  (if (installed_tier_current? seams)
    :current
    (call2 (seam seams :build_tier) ebin root)))

(defn rebuild_tier
  "The CHILD VM's entry: stage the authoritative floor, recheck the installed
   tier, and compile only when still stale. Bootstrap.install!/1 stays in the
   child: its seed initialization must not prestart Env in the parent. The
   parent always reexecutes a fresh VM after the successful child exits."
  []
  (let [ebin (erlang/hd (System/argv))
        root (or (System/get_env "BL_ROOT") (Path/dirname ebin))]
    (rebuild_tier_at ebin root (Map/new))))

; ── the stages ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn rebuild_step!
  "A tier that is stale or mixed generations ends the road for every verb, so it
   is rebuilt BEFORE the application interns it. Self-limiting: the rebuild
   writes the ebin, so the next run's verdict finds a current destination and
   this costs a tier build once per boot-source edit, nothing on a warm tree.
   A re-exec runs `bin/bl` with `BL_BOOT_REEXECED=1`, and a second stale verdict
   after a rebuild REFUSES rather than restarting forever."
  [argv seams ebin root]
  (call1 (seam seams :stderr)
         "bl: the boot tier in build/ is not this tree's generation; rebuilding it as a unit")
  (let [child
        (call3 (seam seams :cmd)
               (call1 (seam seams :find_executable) "elixir")
               (erlang/++ (child_pa (call0 (seam seams :code_path)))
                          (lst "-e" (build_program) "--" ebin))
               (lst (tup :stderr_to_stdout true)
                    (tup :into (IO/stream :stderr :line))))
        status (erlang/element 2 child)]
    (cond
      (erlang/not (erlang/== 0 status))
        (do
          (call1 (seam seams :stderr)
                 (bcat "bl: the boot tier does not rebuild from source (exit "
                       (erlang/integer_to_binary status) "); this image is unusable"))
          (call1 (seam seams :halt) 70))

      (erlang/== "1" (call1 (seam seams :get_env) "BL_BOOT_REEXECED"))
        (do
          (call1 (seam seams :stderr)
                 "bl: boot tier is still stale after a rebuild; refusing another restart")
          (call1 (seam seams :halt) 70))

      :else
        (let [again
              (call3 (seam seams :cmd)
                     (Path/join root "bin/bl")
                     argv
                     (lst (tup :env (lst (tup "BL_BOOT_REEXECED" "1")))
                          (tup :stderr_to_stdout true)
                          (tup :into (IO/stream :stdio :line))))]
          (call1 (seam seams :halt) (erlang/element 2 again))))))

(defn dispatch!
  "The last step: load `bl.cli` and run it on `argv`. Its return value IS the
   process exit code, so the CLI's own failures stay the CLI's."
  [argv seams]
  (call1 (seam seams :ensure_loaded) "bl.cli")
  (let [run (call2 (seam seams :fetch) "bl.cli" "run-argv")]
    (call1 (seam seams :halt) (call2 (seam seams :invoke) run (lst argv)))))

(defn start_app!
  "Start the application and finish the boot. A machine with no libraries
   cannot start it, and one command has to work on exactly such a machine: the
   provisioning verb boots the LANGUAGE without the application (`AOT.boot/0`
   starts `Env` and `Loader.Server` and seeds core) while every other verb
   refuses to run on a half-started image and SAYS SO, with the reason. Once the
   application is up, every `defnative` crate's cdylib is brought current — the
   committed `.so` served the app boot's own LazySeq, this rebuilds only a crate
   whose Rust source changed (`~1ms` when all current; cargo absent reports and
   continues)."
  [argv seams]
  (let [result (call0 (seam seams :start_app))]
    (cond
      (erlang/== :ok (erlang/element 1 result))
        (do
          (call1 (seam seams :ensure_loaded) "vm.native")
          (call2 (seam seams :invoke)
                 (call2 (seam seams :fetch) "vm.native" "build-all!")
                 (lst))
          (dispatch! argv seams))

      (provisioning_argv? argv)
        (do
          (call0 (seam seams :load_app))
          (call1 (seam seams :stderr)
                 (bcat "bl: the application did not start (" (Kernel/inspect result)
                       "); running " (erlang/hd argv)
                       " anyway, because it installs what the application needs"))
          (call0 (seam seams :aot_boot))
          (dispatch! argv seams))

      :else
        (do
          (call1 (seam seams :stderr)
                 (bcat "bl: the application did not start: " (Kernel/inspect result)))
          (call1 (seam seams :stderr)
                 "bl: this image is not usable as-is; `bl build --force` rebuilds it, and `bl doctor` says what it is")
          (call1 (seam seams :halt) 70)))))

; ── the entry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn boot
  "The launcher, for `argv`, with `seams` overriding the effectful edges
   (`main/1` passes an empty map). The order is the whole contract: the pinned
   host ABI is installed BEFORE the tier is judged (the verdict reads beam
   provenance), the tier is rebuilt AS A UNIT before the application interns it,
   and the application is started (or the language alone, for `deps`) before
   `bl.cli` is loaded."
  [argv seams]
  (let [ebin (boot_ebin seams)
        root (boot_root seams ebin)]
    (call1 (seam seams :install_hosts) ebin)
    (let [manifest (call0 (seam seams :manifest))
          key_now (call0 (seam seams :key_now))
          namespaces (call0 (seam seams :boot_namespaces))
          pairs (Enum/map namespaces
                  (fn [ns] (tup ns (call1 (seam seams :provenance) (tier_module ns)))))
          can_judge? (and (Code/ensure_loaded? :"Elixir.BeamLisp.AOT")
                          (erlang/function_exported :"Elixir.BeamLisp.AOT" :beam_provenance 1))]
      (if (tier_verdict manifest can_judge? pairs key_now)
        (start_app! argv seams)
        (rebuild_step! argv seams ebin root)))))

(defn main
  "The trampoline entry. `main/0` is this VM's own argv; `main/1` takes an
   explicit one. Both run the flow with the REAL seams — the override map is
   the test's door, not a production one."
  ([] (main (System/argv)))
  ([argv] (boot argv (Map/new))))
